  "narrative": "The response-deadline validation for H80 confirms the overall CDKN2A-loss–MDM2 association in the supplied dataset. Because H80 has direction -1, the positive signed estimates correspond to a negative raw exposed-minus-comparator difference: CDKN2A-loss lines have more negative MDM2 dependency scores, i.e. stronger MDM2 dependency. Discovery R59 = +0.117 [0.082, 0.152] and independent validation R66 = +0.130 [0.078, 0.182] are concordant, and the validation lower bound is well above both zero and the approximate 10% clinical threshold for MDM2 (about 0.027, using the observed MDM2 score scale). H80 therefore remains accepted and closed as one of the main supported dataset-specific associations.\n\nThe newly delivered validation for H85 changes the interpretation of the MTAP-loss-specific CDKN2A stratum. Discovery R83 showed a large clinically meaningful signed effect, +0.165 [0.081, 0.249], but the independent validation R92 was attenuated and inconclusive: +0.096 [-0.002, 0.195]. The validation point estimate remains positive and below the discovery estimate, with overlapping uncertainty, but the independent interval now crosses zero and includes values below the 10% clinical threshold. Because the interval was constructed for independent validation, this should not be treated as a simple confirmation. I therefore downgrade H85 from accepted to unresolved active. This does not reject a CDKN2A effect in MTAP-loss tumors; it says that the subgroup-specific claim is not sufficiently supported after validation. The correct conclusion is that CDKN2A loss is robustly associated with stronger MDM2 dependency overall, while whether this association is especially strong, absent, or modified in MTAP-loss tumors remains uncertain until the direct interaction test and related co-deletion analyses are evaluated.\n\nThe current supported findings are dataset-specific associations, not causal or externally generalizable claims. Five core associations remain: MSI-high predicts much stronger WRN dependency (H6, validated); SMARCA4 loss predicts much stronger SMARCA2 dependency (H7, validated); TP53 loss predicts much weaker USP7 dependency (H11, validated); KEAP1 loss predicts much weaker NFE2L2 dependency (H17, validated; the original opposite-direction claim H8 is rejected); and CDKN2A loss predicts stronger MDM2 dependency overall (H80, validated). The MDM2 effect is additionally supported in several accepted strata, including Avana and KY libraries, TP53-intact and TP53-loss tumors, MTAP-intact tumors, and tumors with low 9p21 copy number. These strata reduce concern that the primary H80 association is confined to one screening platform or one co-alteration context. They do not settle mechanistic attribution to CDKN2A alone versus broader 9p21 deletion structure; H86 and H108 are the key interaction tests still needed.\n\nNegative and specificity findings are also informative. Role-free marker D/E/F associations with MDM2 and USP7 were rejected, supporting assay specificity rather than broad marker structure. CDKN2A loss was not associated with meaningful USP7 or POLR2A shifts, supporting selective association with MDM2 among the tested outcomes. MTAP loss among CDKN2A-intact tumors was null, arguing against MTAP alone driving the primary CDKN2A-MDM2 signal. Broad biological follow-ups were negative: MYC amplification did not show a clinically meaningful KIF18A shift, ERBB2 amplification did not show a TMED10 shift, and TP53 loss did not show an RPL11 shift; the latter two or three tests strengthen specificity for the much larger SMARCA4-SMARCA2, TP53-USP7, and KEAP1-NFE2L2 associations.\n\nRemaining unresolved uncertainty includes EGFR amplification, the small overall non-Avana CDKN2A-MDM2 stratum, MTAP-loss-specific CDKN2A-MDM2 interaction, MTAP overall, MTAP within low-9p21 tumors, and additional library/context robustness for TP53-USP7 and KEAP1-NFE2L2. The most informative next analyses are direct interaction and co-deletion tests: H86 CDKN2A-by-MTAP interaction, H81 MTAP overall, H108 CDKN2A-by-low-9p21 interaction, H109 MTAP within low-9p21 tumors, and related stratified follow-ups. These should resolve whether the robust CDKN2A-MDM2 association is best interpreted as broadly CDKN2A-associated, modified by MTAP co-loss, or a marker for broader 9p21 deletion biology.",
  "research_notes": "Current accepted dataset-specific associations: H6 MSI-high -> much stronger WRN dependency; H7 SMARCA4 loss -> much stronger SMARCA2 dependency; H11 TP53 loss -> much weaker USP7 dependency; H17 KEAP1 loss -> much weaker NFE2L2 dependency; H80 CDKN2A loss -> stronger MDM2 dependency overall, validated. MDM2 robust accepted strata: H92 Avana, H107 KY, H82 TP53-intact, H83 TP53-loss, H84 MTAP-intact, H100 MTAP/TP53-intact, H101 low copy_number_9p21. H85 MTAP-loss CDKN2A stratum is now unresolved active: R83 +0.165 [0.081,0.249], independent validation R92 +0.096 [-0.002,0.195]; independent interval crosses null and 10% threshold, so do not claim MTAP-loss-specific effect without direct interaction evidence. H93 overall non-Avana CDKN2A remains borderline unresolved; H99 MTAP within CDKN2A-loss unresolved; H81 MTAP overall active. Negative specificity/control findings: role-free markers null for MDM2/USP7/WRN/SMARCA2/NFE2L2; CDKN2A-USP7 and CDKN2A-POLR2A null; MTAP among CDKN2A-intact null; MYC-KIF18A, ERBB2-TMED10, and TP53-RPL11 null; broad RB1-KIF18A, NF1-STAT3, ERBB2-EGFR nulls. EGFR amplification remains deferred/unresolved. Next most informative: H86 CDKN2A-by-MTAP interaction, H81 MTAP overall, H108 CDKN2A-by-low9p21 interaction, H109 MTAP within low9p21, plus remaining context controls for USP7/NFE2L2. All claims are associations in the supplied dataset only; no causal or external-generalization interpretation."
