 "narrative": "The new results mostly support the robustness of the already accepted pathway associations and add useful, although not definitive, context for the CDKN2A-MDM2 attribution problem. I used the supplied dataset only and did not interpret these as causal or externally generalizable. For direction -1 claims, positive signed estimates mean the exposed group has a raw more negative, i.e. stronger, dependency score; for direction +1 claims, positive signed estimates mean the exposed group has a raw less negative, i.e. weaker, dependency score. The approximate 10% clinical scale for MDM2 is about 0.027, so any MDM2 effect interpretation should focus on estimates and intervals relative to this threshold and their uncertainty.\n\nH127 tests CDKN2A loss in tumors with more severe low copy_number_9p21 (<= -0.75). The estimate is +0.122 with interval [0.054, 0.191], entirely above zero and above the MDM2 threshold, so I accept this as a dataset-specific stratum association: even among more severely 9p21-low tumors, CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency. However, the comparator cell has only 29 observations, and eligibility on copy_number_9p21 is strongly entangled with CDKN2A-loss biology. I therefore leave the investigation active rather than treating this as a fully settled attribution test; it supports broadness but does not fully separate CDKN2A from neighboring 9p21 deletion structure.\n\nH128 is not informative because the comparison cell is insufficient: only five CDKN2A-loss tumors satisfy copy_number_9p21 >= -0.25. The null estimate should not be read as evidence against CDKN2A-MDM2 association in higher-copy tumors; the design simply cannot test it reliably. I mark it unresolved and deferred.\n\nThe MTAP tests are suggestive but imprecise. H129 asks whether MTAP loss has residual association with MDM2 dependency within CDKN2A-loss and low-copy_number_9p21 tumors; H130 asks the CDKN2A-intact counterpart; H131 directly tests the MTAP-by-low-9p21 interaction. All are positive-signed but have intervals crossing zero and crossing or approaching the clinical threshold: H129 +0.069 [-0.024, 0.162], H130 +0.039 [-0.020, 0.098], H131 +0.063 [-0.012, 0.138]. These are compatible with no MTAP modification, with a modest effect, or with an effect near/above the 10% scale. I keep H129 and H131 active because they are central to attribution. H130 is small and lower information because only 27 MTAP-loss tumors are in the CDKN2A-intact low-9p21 eligible set, so I defer it rather than conclude either way.\n\nH132 is more decisive as a dataset-specific stratum: CDKN2A loss within MTAP-loss and TP53-intact tumors gives +0.162 [0.070, 0.255]. This is large, excludes the approximate MDM2 threshold, and suggests that the apparent MTAP-loss attenuation of the CDKN2A-MDM2 association is not explained simply by TP53-loss composition. I accept it but request validation for H132 because it is a selected refinement after several CDKN2A-MDM2 tests and is directly relevant to the main unresolved attribution issue. Independent confirmation would guard against multiplicity and small-stratum instability while preserving the distinction between dataset association and causality.\n\nThe context-robustness results strongly support the accepted core associations rather than creating new claims. In STK11-loss tumors, TP53 loss still shows a very large weaker-USP7 association, H133 +0.825 [0.766, 0.885], and KEAP1 loss still shows a very large weaker-NFE2L2 association, H134 +0.775 [0.727, 0.823]. Under lower Cas9 activity (<=70%), TP53-USP7 (H135 +0.798 [0.757, 0.839]), KEAP1-NFE2L2 (H136 +0.797 [0.754, 0.841]), and SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2 dependency (H137 +0.773 [0.732, 0.814]) remain enormous and precisely estimated. I accept and close these as robustness strata; they are not needed as new headline effects, but they reduce concerns that the main associations are artifacts of common co-alterations or screen efficiency.\n\nFinally, H138 shows no clinically meaningful SMARCA2 effect for STK11 loss: +0.0018 [-0.039, 0.042], far below the approximate SMARCA2 10% scale and compatible with zero. I reject and close it, which supports SMARCA4-specific SMARCA2 dependency rather than a broad effect of common lung co-alterations on SMARCA2. Overall, the accepted core associations remain unchanged in magnitude and specificity, and the only new validation request is for H132 because it is the most important new threshold-relevant attribution stratum.",
 "research_notes": "Core accepted dataset-specific associations remain MSI-high with much stronger WRN dependency (H6), SMARCA4 loss with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency (H7), TP53 loss with much weaker USP7 dependency (H11), KEAP1 loss with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency (H17), and CDKN2A loss with stronger MDM2 dependency overall (H80). Additional accepted strata continue to support platform/context robustness: WRN strata across libraries/batches/Cas9/mutation burden; SMARCA2 strata including Avana, non-Avana, batch, TP53-intact, CDKN2A-loss, and low-Cas9; KEAP1-NFE2L2 strata including Avana, non-Avana, CDKN2A-loss, STK11-loss, and low-Cas9; TP53-USP7 strata including CDKN2A-loss, STK11-loss, and low-Cas9. Role-free marker nulls and SMARCA4 specificity nulls support outcome specificity.\n\nNew appraise results: H127 accepted but left active because the severe low-9p21 CDKN2A-MDM2 stratum is clinically large but has a small comparator cell (n=29) and remains entangled with deletion structure. H128 is uninformative due to insufficient cell size and is deferred. H132 accepted and active with validation requested because CDKN2A loss in MTAP-loss/TP53-intact tumors has a large interval above the MDM2 threshold and is important for the CDKN2A-MDM2 attribution issue. H133-H137 accepted closed as very large context-robustness strata. H138 rejected closed because STK11 loss has no SMARCA2 effect, supporting paralog-specific SMARCA4 specificity.\n\nThe main unresolved biology remains CDKN2A-MDM2 attribution by MTAP co-loss and 9p21 deletion structure. H85, H86, H99, H109, H129, H131 and H132 are the relevant thread; H130 and H128 are low-information deferred tests. H127 broadens the CDKN2A-MDM2 association to severe 9p21-low contexts but does not fully separate CDKN2A from neighboring deletion effects. Do not request validation for already validated huge core effects unless a new claim has threshold-relevant uncertainty. All conclusions remain associations in the supplied d