  "narrative": "The strongest supported patterns are context-specific CRISPR dependencies: MSI-high is associated with much stronger WRN dependency, SMARCA4 loss with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency, TP53 loss with much weaker USP7 dependency, KEAP1 loss with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency, and CDKN2A loss with stronger MDM2 dependency. The MDM2 attribution work now favors CDKN2A-specific rather than MTAP co-deletion as the dominant signal when MTAP is intact, while direct MTAP modification tests remain sparse or underpowered. I therefore defer further MTAP interaction strata and instead prioritize tests of whether these accepted associations are robust across technical screen-quality, matched omics availability, proliferation context, and genome-copy-number context. These are useful because screen sensitivity, CRISPR activity, cell growth, and ploidy can affect apparent dependency scores and because high-quality matched-QC strata are important for internal validity. The high-ploidy CDKN2A-MDM2 test is explicitly uncertain: aneuploidy might amplify, attenuate, or leave unchanged the CDKN2A-MDM2 association. I also register two new pathway-focused STAT3 hypotheses. EGFR and ERBB2 amplifications can activate STAT3-associated signaling, so if STAT3 is a downstream effector in those contexts, EGFR- or ERBB2-amplified cells may have stronger STAT3 dependency, meaning a more negative dependency score and a negative raw exposed-minus-comparator difference. These are dataset-specific associations and do not establish causality or external generalizability.",
